<Project>

  <!--
    Applies to Tests/LancacheManager.Tests ONLY. MSBuild stops at the first Directory.Build.props it
    finds walking up from a project, and Api/Directory.Build.props sits closer to
    Api/LancacheManager/LancacheManager.csproj - so nothing in this file reaches the API.

    AnalysisMode=Recommended raises the .NET analyzer set above the default. It is what surfaces
    CA1859 (interface parameter/return where the concrete type is always passed) rather than only
    the highest-confidence cases. Per-rule severity overrides live in .editorconfig and still win
    over this setting.

    Style rules are not enforced at build time here: EnforceCodeStyleInBuild is left off, so IDE1006
    naming violations stay warnings in the test project and remain a `dotnet format` concern.
    The API does the opposite - Api/Directory.Build.props turns EnforceCodeStyleInBuild on and puts
    IDE1006 in WarningsAsErrors, so an async method there that lacks the Async suffix fails the
    build. Do not read this file as describing the API's settings.

    TreatWarningsAsErrors makes every remaining warning fail the build. The test project ships
    nowhere, so there is no release build to protect, and the analyzer families that fire here -
    CA (AnalysisMode above), xUnit (the xunit.analyzers package) and the compiler's own CS set -
    are all things a test author should fix rather than push. Style rules stay out of it because
    EnforceCodeStyleInBuild is off, so this does not drag in IDE1006. The API cannot do the same:
    it is compiled inside the Docker image builds, so it promotes named rules instead.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AnalysisMode>Recommended</AnalysisMode>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
